> Original seven-node sine reconstruction

Retain seven uniform nodes from0 through2*pi and the original301-point figure
grid, including both endpoints. The native study builds these exact prescribed
grids, samples sin at each node, owns one once-validated interpolator and
evaluates it on the full query grid. The analytic sine is an explicit reference,
not data generated by another interpolation package. Every plotted reconstructed
value comes from the actual native query API.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/linear_interp_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto s = dm::algorithms::linear_interpolation_sine_study(7, 300);
    std::print("| original nodes | original figure query points | actual maximum grid error | smooth-sine " "h-squared-over8 reference |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| {} | {} | {:.12f} | {:.12f} " "|\n",
               s.xs.size(), s.queries.size(), s.max_grid_error,
               s.error_reference);
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(s.queries, s.exact, "analytic sin", {16, 185, 129}, 2).line(
        s.queries, s.interpolated, "actual linear interpolant", {220, 38, 38}, 2).points(s.xs, s.ys, "original7 samples", {37, 99, 235}, 6).y_limits(-1.3, 1.7).title("Original seven-node sine reconstruction").x_label("x in radians").y_label("function or interpolated value").save_svg("build/linear_interp_original.svg");
}
```
